  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 1153 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nMuch cooler for mid week with frost/light freezes looking more\nlikely.\n\nCooler conditions for Sunday Night and Monday. Surface high\npressure ridge drops into the area for Sunday night, and will\nallow wind speeds to drop off. Lows will drop into the mid 30s \nacross much of the area, with the first frost of the season\npossible. A few areas across the northern Big Country may flirt\nwith a light freeze.\n\nMonday Night into Tuesday morning looks a little more interesting.\nModels are showing a little sharper upper trough set to move\nacross the area, and have brought a little precip back into the\narea from 06Z-18Z Tuesday. Most models show temperatures in the\nupper 30s with some very spotty rainfall possible. Will need to\nkeep an eye on the RRFS however which shows a little more\nconcentrated area of precip, enough to wet bulb some temperatures\ndown into the low/mid 30s and produce a little wintry mix\npossible. This would be mainly across the higher elevations of \nthe Concho Valley north into the hills south of Sweetwater and \nAbilene where the extra 500 hundred feet or more of elevation may\nmake a difference. Not going to add a wintry mix to the forecast \nquite yet off just this mornings run of the RRFS, but will be \nworth keeping an eye on.\n\nSurface high builds into the area for Wednesday and Thursday,\nsetting the stages for the first freeze of the season for many\nlocations. Current forecasts show lower 30s across the area, and\nare likely not handling the radiational cooling set up that is\npossible. Little early to tell which morning may be the coldest\nquite yet, but would not ne surprised to see many areas briefly dropping\ninto the upper 20s at least 1 of the 2 mornings. \n\nModels still having their differences with the track of the next\nupper system for late week (Friday-Saturday), with the GFS still\nmore closed off and slower, and the ECMWF faster and weaker. In\nthe end, not sure how much difference it will end up making in the\nactual day to day weather and thus not going to deviate from the\nmodel blends at this point.",
